{
  "teamId": 138,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 St. Louis Cardinals were a team of wild swings and missed opportunities, a club talented enough to win 88 games yet fall just short of October baseball. They opened the year with immediate turbulence: a pair of ugly April blown saves by Dominic Leone against Milwaukee set a tone of bullpen fragility that would haunt them all season. Yet the Cardinals steadied themselves through April and May, leaning on clutch moments from José Martínez, Matt Carpenter, and a Dexter Fowler walk-off to keep themselves in contention.\n\nJune and early July told a harder story. The bullpen continued to falter — Matt Bowman, Bud Norris, and Jordan Hicks all featured in costly meltdowns — and the team stumbled to a 12-15 June and 12-14 July. The low point came on June 18, when Aaron Altherr's two-run double off Bowman swung the win probability by a stunning 0.813 in Philadelphia's favor. The Cardinals were a team leaking runs at the worst moments, and the standings reflected it.\n\nThen came August — one of the most dominant single-month performances in recent Cardinals history. The team went 22-6, an almost otherworldly stretch propelled by Carpenter's prolific home run pace, big contributions from Paul DeJong, and a lineup that suddenly clicked. José Martínez's two-run single on August 2 was emblematic of the surge: calm, clutch, and decisive. For a few weeks, the Cardinals looked like a legitimate contender and playoff lock.\n\nBut September cruelly unraveled the dream. A 12-15 finish, punctuated by Bud Norris surrendering home runs to Eugenio Suarez and Bryce Harper in back-to-back games, extinguished the playoff push. The bullpen that had been a liability all year simply could not hold leads when it mattered most. Tyler O'Neill's September home run offered a glimpse of the future, and Patrick Wisdom's late grand slam added some consolation power, but the Cardinals finished just outside the wild card, left to wonder what might have been if August had come a month sooner.",
